      "latency_ms": 2726.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 536,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid:** (Rivers, oceans, rain)\n  * **Solid:** (Ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas:** (Water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unusual and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salts, sugars, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of plants).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the internal temperature of living organisms.\n* **Expanding When Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands when it turns from a liquid into a solid (ice). This causes ice to be less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes, forms clouds in the atmosphere, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into bodies of water.\n* **The Basis of Life:** Water makes up about 60% of the adult human body and about 71% of the Earth’s surface. Without water, biochemical reactions necessary for life (like metabolism and digestion) cannot take place. \n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the driving force of nature and the cradle of life."
